<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle · Siege</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Nalapani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1814">1814</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Khalanga, Siege of Kalunga · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-nepalese-war">Anglo-Nepalese War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Nalapani was the first major engagement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-nepalese-war">Anglo-Nepalese War</a>, fought in October and November 1814 at the hill fort of Khalanga (Kalunga) near Dehradun. A small Gorkhali garrison under Balbhadra Kunwar repulsed repeated British assaults, killing General Robert <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rollo">Rollo</a> Gillespie, before abandoning the fort when its water ran out. Both sides honoured the defenders with memorials.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-nalapani.jpg" alt="Battle of Nalapani" width="320" height="243" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Henry Thompson · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Death_of_Rollo_Gillespie_(Cassell%27s_illustrated_history_of_India).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1814, under the Governor-General Francis Edward Rawdon-Hastings, Earl of Moira, long-standing disputes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> and the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> turned to open war. The British planned to attack on two fronts along a frontier of more than 1,500 km, and the division formed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meerut">Meerut</a> under Major-General Robert Rollo Gillespie, 3,513 men in all, was the first to cross into Nepalese territory, marching on Dehra Dun.</p><p>Knowing he could not hold the town, Captain Balbhadra Kunwar withdrew with about 600 people, dependents included, to the small hill fort of Nalapani. His force included Magar soldiers of the Purano Gorakh Battalion and men recruited in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garhwal">Garhwal</a>. The fort stood on a hill of 500 to 600 feet covered in dense jungle, and its walls were still unfinished, strengthened with stockades of stones and stakes. Balbhadra tore up a British summons to surrender.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The first assault came on 31 October 1814, a day before the official declaration of war. Gillespie planned to storm the fort with four columns, but only two moved at his signal, which was given hours early, and they were cut down by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> fire, arrows and stones. Rallying his men within 30 yards of the gate, Gillespie was shot dead. The British lost 32 dead and 228 wounded that day.</p><p>Colonel Sebright Mawbey withdrew to Dehra until heavy guns arrived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>. On 27 November a section of the northern wall fell, but two charges into the breach were repelled, at a cost of 37 dead and over 443 wounded. The British then encircled the fort and cut off its water. On 30 November Balbhadra, refusing to surrender, broke out with about 70 survivors into the hills. Upwards of 90 bodies were found inside.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>British accounts praised the garrison's conduct; the traveller James Baillie Fraser told of a Nepalese soldier with a shattered jaw who came to the British batteries for treatment and, once healed, asked to return to fight. The long siege also encouraged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-confederacy">Maratha Confederacy</a> in its struggle against the British. The British later built two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obelisk">obelisks</a> at Nalapani, one for Gillespie and one inscribed to &quot;Our brave adversary Bul Buddur and his gallant men&quot;. Balbhadra became a national hero in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, and the battle helped lead to the recruitment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurkhas">Gurkhas</a> into the British forces.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Nalapani” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nepali troops inflicted heavy losses at hill forts such as <b>Kalunga</b>, but British forces under David Ochterlony eventually prevailed, and the Treaty of Sugauli cost Nepal about a third of its territory.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Kingdom of Nepal · Military</a></li></ul></section>
